{
  "_comment_listen": "TCP host:port the relay's websocket listener binds to. Bind to LOOPBACK ONLY -- Caddy (deploy/relay/Caddyfile) is the only thing that should reach this port, and the relay authenticates nothing at the transport layer (internal/remote/relay/server.go: Start does net.Listen(\"tcp\", cfg.Listen) unconditionally). Must match the reverse_proxy target in the Caddyfile.",
  "listen": "127.0.0.1:9440",

  "_comment_admin_listen": "R2 playbook 6.5 health/readiness surface: /healthz (process up) and /readyz (bbolt writable, public listener accepting, free disk above disk_free_min_bytes below) on a SEPARATE loopback-only port from `listen` above. Start refuses a non-loopback value outright -- this is NEVER the public listener, and the public protocol gains no unauthenticated endpoint from it (the same doctor rule playbook 6.5 states for the diagnostic capability applies to health too). deploy/relay/docker-compose.yml's healthcheck polls this port.",
  "admin_listen": "127.0.0.1:9441",

  "_comment_tls_mode": "Parsed by relay.LoadConfig but READ BY NOTHING THAT SERVES: Start binds Listen as plain ws:// unconditionally regardless of this value (docs/operations/relay-runbook.md section 0; internal/remote/relay/config.go's own field comment says the same). It does not turn TLS on or off. Caddy is the entire TLS story for this deployment. Left \"off\" so this file does not claim a protection it does not provide.",
  "tls_mode": "off",

  "_comment_db_path": "Path to the relay's bbolt persistence file (mailbox items, push tokens, pairing/consent state). Must be on writable storage that survives a reboot -- point it at the systemd unit's StateDirectory (deploy/relay/swarm-relay.service sets StateDirectory=swarm-relay, i.e. /var/lib/swarm-relay).",
  "db_path": "/var/lib/swarm-relay/relay.db",

  "_comment_operator_secret_file": "R2 playbook 6.5: path where the relay persists a generated high-entropy operator secret (0600) the first time it boots and finds none there yet -- 'diagnostic/admin authority', consumed by the `swarm relay doctor` capability, and explicitly NOT a substitute for Web-PKI server authentication. Point it at the same StateDirectory as db_path so it survives a restart instead of regenerating (which would invalidate every capability the doctor had minted). The secret itself is never logged by this binary. Omit this key entirely to leave diagnostics disabled.",
  "operator_secret_file": "/var/lib/swarm-relay/operator.secret",

  "_comment_push_gateway": "AMENDED BY ADR-015 (2026-08-15): push moves off the relay to a Swarm-operated gateway -- Android registers with the gateway directly and swarm-remote submits the wake. The relay's own push transport (a push_credentials key naming a Google service-account JSON document) is the LEGACY path, retained only for a pairing whose push_transport has not yet migrated from legacy_relay to gateway (playbook section 12). It is not shown in this example because a new deployment should not configure it. If you are still inside that compatibility window, see docs/operations/relay-vps-deploy.md section 4a for the field and its file-permission requirements, and docs/operations/metadata-disclosure.md sections 1a/2 for what it discloses.",

  "_comment_trusted_proxies": "R2 playbook 6.5 trusted-proxy source identity. CIDRs whose connections are Caddy, never a real client. Caddy is the ONLY thing that reaches this loopback listener (see the listen comment above), so 127.0.0.1/32 covers it exactly. With a peer in this list, the relay reads the client identity for per-source quotas from the LAST (rightmost) X-Forwarded-For hop -- the one entry Caddy itself appends -- instead of the raw TCP peer, which would otherwise be Caddy for every real client (see the quotas comment below). Leave this list empty only if you are NOT fronting the relay with a reverse proxy. TRUST BOUNDARY: nothing enforces that Caddy is really the only thing on the other end -- ANY local process able to reach 127.0.0.1:9440 (i.e. any local user on this host, not just Caddy) is inside this trust CIDR and can name its own quota bucket with a single forged X-Forwarded-For header; that is an accepted v1 risk on a single-tenant VPS, not a gap this file can close. RESOLVED FOR THE COMPOSE BUNDLE (R2-A, deploy/relay/docker-compose.yml, bead agents-tracker-hggx.3.1): the concern this note used to raise -- Caddy reaching the relay over a bridge network address this CIDR would not cover -- does not apply to the shipped bundle. docker-compose.yml deliberately puts Caddy in swarm-relay's OWN network namespace (`network_mode: \"service:swarm-relay\"`, its own header comment explains why) rather than a separate bridge network, specifically so Caddy connects to 127.0.0.1 exactly as it does in this host topology -- this file (relay.config.example) and the Caddyfile are shared, unmodified, between both deployment paths, and 127.0.0.1/32 below covers Caddy under Compose exactly as it does under systemd. If you deploy a DIFFERENT Compose topology (a separate bridge network, Caddy reached by service-name DNS), this CIDR will not cover it, and the fix is trusted_proxies naming Caddy's actual bridge address as its own narrow /32 -- never the whole bridge subnet, which would let any other container on it spoof its way into an arbitrary bucket.",
  "trusted_proxies": ["127.0.0.1/32"],

  "_comment_handshake_timeout": "Nanoseconds (time.Duration marshals as an int64 count of nanoseconds). Bounds how long an UNAUTHENTICATED connection may sit idle before the relay closes it (slowloris defense). 30000000000 = 30s, the shipped default.",
  "handshake_timeout": 30000000000,

  "_comment_presence_timeout": "Nanoseconds. How long after a gateway disconnect presence flips to offline and the silent-push path fires. 30000000000 = 30s, the shipped default.",
  "presence_timeout": 30000000000,

  "_comment_rendezvous_ttl": "Nanoseconds. Hard lifetime of a pairing rendezvous slot. 60000000000 = 60s, the shipped default.",
  "rendezvous_ttl": 60000000000,

  "_comment_max_server_wait": "Nanoseconds. Ceiling on a parked mailbox_wait before the relay answers a clean empty page instead of holding the socket open. 0 selects the built-in default (25s), deliberately under the 30-60s idle timeout common to intermediaries -- including a reverse proxy like Caddy -- so the relay always answers before the proxy would sever the connection.",
  "max_server_wait": 0,

  "_comment_retention_cap": "Nanoseconds. Mailbox items older than this are purged even if never acknowledged. 604800000000000 = 7 days, the shipped default.",
  "retention_cap": 604800000000000,

  "_comment_sweep_interval": "Nanoseconds. Cadence of the background maintenance sweep (presence-went-silent pushes + retention purges). cmd/swarm-relay defaults this to 30s itself when the config leaves it at 0 or omits it, so this line is redundant with the binary's own default -- kept explicit so the cadence is visible without reading main.go. 30000000000 = 30s.",
  "sweep_interval": 30000000000,

  "_comment_quotas": "Day-one abuse controls (R-REL.8), all operator-tunable. Every window below is per minute unless noted otherwise. These values are the relay's own generous shipped defaults (relay.DefaultConfig) -- tighten deliberately, and read mailbox_append_per_min's comment before touching that one specifically. NOTE ON CADDY: with trusted_proxies set (above) to cover Caddy's address, the relay derives every one of these per-source windows from the client address it recovers from X-Forwarded-For, not from the raw TCP peer (which would otherwise be Caddy for every real client, collapsing them into one shared bucket). This is what makes max_concurrent_connections_per_source and conn_per_min mean \"per real client\" again behind this proxy (R2 playbook 6.5). If you ever clear trusted_proxies, these per-source windows revert to keying off Caddy itself for every client combined.",
  "quotas": {
    "_comment_max_concurrent_rendezvous": "Cap on live pairing rendezvous slots at once, across all clients.",
    "max_concurrent_rendezvous": 1024,

    "_comment_max_concurrent_connections": "Global cap on live websocket connections admitted at once. The (cap+1)th connection is refused cleanly, not queued.",
    "max_concurrent_connections": 4096,

    "_comment_max_concurrent_connections_per_source": "Same cap, scoped to one transport source -- with trusted_proxies covering Caddy (above), \"one source\" means one real client behind it, not Caddy itself.",
    "max_concurrent_connections_per_source": 64,

    "_comment_mailbox_append_per_min": "Appends per target routing id per minute. MUST STAY >= 600: this is the ONLY cap that applies to live typing (ops_per_min explicitly excludes mailbox_append), the client coalesces to <= 8 frames/s sustained, and 600/min is the deliberate 20% headroom over that. A lower value silently breaks live typing with no error the user ever sees.",
    "mailbox_append_per_min": 600,

    "_comment_mailbox_max_items": "Per-mailbox depth cap: an append past this many un-acked items is refused (not dropped) until the device drains. Bounds unbounded growth from a device that never reads; capacity recovers on ack.",
    "mailbox_max_items": 10000,

    "_comment_push_per_min": "Push triggers per target routing id per minute. AMENDED BY ADR-015 (2026-08-15): this quota governs the legacy relay-hosted push transport only -- the Swarm-operated gateway enforces its own per-address and per-source quotas once a pairing migrates (playbook section 12).",
    "push_per_min": 600,

    "_comment_conn_per_min": "Pre-authentication auth_init attempts per transport source per minute. See the Caddy note above.",
    "conn_per_min": 600,

    "_comment_ops_per_min": "Per-source cap on every other state-touching op (auth_resp, authorize_device, mailbox_read/ack, token_register/delete, presence, device_revoke, the rendezvous ops). mailbox_append and push_trigger keep their own dedicated windows above. AMENDED BY ADR-015 (2026-08-15): token_register/delete and push_trigger are the legacy relay-hosted push ops -- they stay on the schedule of the push_transport compatibility window (playbook section 12) and leave the protocol once every pairing has migrated to the gateway.",
    "ops_per_min": 600,

    "_comment_disk_free_min_bytes": "R2 playbook 6.5 low-disk alarm: /readyz on admin_listen above refuses (503) once free space on db_path's filesystem falls below this many bytes, and the relay logs one bounded warning per transition into the low state (never once per poll). 1073741824 = 1 GiB, the shipped default -- generous for any real VPS disk, low enough to fire well before bbolt writes start failing outright. A value <= 0 disables the check.",
    "disk_free_min_bytes": 1073741824
  }
}
